  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure surface and aloft will provide dry, and increasingly \nmild conditions this week. Valley fog will be possible in the \nmornings, although it should generally become less dense and \npersistent with each passing day. Main highlight much of this week \nwill be the increasingly dry air aloft, with low afternoon RH, \nparticularly across the mountainous counties. Thankfully, winds \nshould remain relatively light, mitigating much in terms of fire \nweather concerns, although of course, with the dry conditions a few \nbrush fires are likely, but no fire weather headlines are \nanticipated. Continued the trend of lowering afternoon dew points \nbelow much of the guidance. \n\n\nThe next chance for precipitation doesn't arrive until late in the \nweek or early next week. A low will move northeast along the \nAtlantic coast over the weekend. Much of this moisture looks to miss \nus at this point, however, an approaching trough from the west \nduring the period looks more promising in terms of light \nprecipitation, along with a return of gusty winds, and cooler \nconditions.",
